# What Does "implements" Do on a Class?

When you see the `implements` keyword in PHP, it signals a specific contract between your class and an interface. Unlike `extends`, which allows a class to inherit properties and methods from a parent class, `implements` requires a class to adhere to a predefined set of method signatures defined by an interface. This mechanism enforces polymorphism without coupling the class to a concrete implementation.

Consider the scenario where you are building a logging system. You might define an `ILogger` interface with a single method:

```php
interface ILogger {
    public function log(string $message): void;
}
```

If you have a `FileLogger` and a `DatabaseLogger`, neither can extend each other because they serve different purposes. However, both can `implement` the `ILogger` interface. This forces every implementing class to provide a concrete definition for the `log()` method. The language runtime will throw a fatal error if you attempt to declare a class as implementing an interface without defining all required methods.

This constraint is powerful because it decouples the consumer of the service from the producer. In your application code, you can declare a dependency on the `ILogger` type rather than a specific logger implementation. This allows you to swap implementations seamlessly, perhaps switching from file-based logging to database logging without altering the core business logic.

The technical distinction between `extends` and `implements` often trips up developers coming from languages like Java or C#. In PHP, a class can `extend` only one parent class due to single inheritance rules, but it can `implement` multiple interfaces simultaneously. This is known as multiple interface implementation. For example:

```php
interface Flyable {
    public function fly(): void;
}

interface Swimmable {
    public function swim(): void;
}

class Duck implements Flyable, Swimmable {
    public function fly(): void {
        echo "Flying over the pond";
    }

    public function swim(): void {
        echo "Swimming in the pond";
    }
}
```

Here, `Duck` aggregates behaviors from two distinct contracts. This is often more flexible than deep inheritance hierarchies, which can lead to fragile code where a class inherits too many responsibilities (the God Object problem). By using `implements`, you compose behavior rather than inheriting it.

A common pitfall occurs when developers confuse the syntax with intent. Just because a class has a method that looks identical to one in an interface does not mean it implements the interface. The method must be explicitly declared via the `implements` keyword, and the signature must match exactly. Modifying the visibility of a method or changing the return type without adjusting the interface definition will break this contract.

Furthermore, remember that interfaces do not have properties by default in PHP (prior to version 8.1 where read-only properties were introduced for interfaces). If an interface requires state, you must use getter/setter methods or rely on traits to inject that logic into the implementing class. This separation of concerns keeps your interfaces thin and focused purely on behavior contracts.

If you are working within the Laravel ecosystem, this pattern is ubiquitous. Service providers often define interfaces for repositories, queues, or event handlers, allowing the framework or third-party packages to inject specific implementations at runtime. While `laravelcompany.com` discusses various architectural patterns, the core principle of using interfaces to define contracts remains a foundational best practice for maintainable codebases.

Ultimately, `implements` is not just syntactic sugar; it is a structural tool that enforces modularity. It ensures that your classes behave in predictable ways regardless of their internal implementation details, making your software more robust against changes and easier to test via dependency injection.
